WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.189
Decision science helps people make clearer, more explicit, and more reviewable choices under uncertainty.

2
00:00:07.189 --> 00:00:09.924
It is not a promise of perfect prediction.

3
00:00:09.924 --> 00:00:18.598
It is a discipline for improving the quality of the decision before the outcome is known, then learning from what happens afterward.

4
00:00:18.598 --> 00:00:21.802
Strong decision practice starts with the frame.

5
00:00:21.802 --> 00:00:24.772
Define the real choice, not just the symptom.

6
00:00:24.772 --> 00:00:32.039
Clarify the scope, constraints, stakeholders, time horizon, decision owner, and consequences of inaction.

7
00:00:32.039 --> 00:00:41.026
Then clarify objectives, values, and criteria so the group knows what it is trying to optimize and what tradeoffs it is willing to accept.

8
00:00:41.026 --> 00:00:46.027
Good decision makers generate realistic options and avoid false binaries.

9
00:00:46.027 --> 00:00:51.731
They compare tradeoffs, opportunity costs, reversibility, and staged commitments.

10
00:00:51.731 --> 00:01:01.656
They make uncertainty explicit with probabilities, base rates, forecasts, confidence ranges, scenario thinking, and updates as evidence changes.

11
00:01:01.656 --> 00:01:09.392
They consider risk as likelihood, impact, context, downside, upside, tail exposure, mitigation, and contingency.

12
00:01:09.392 --> 00:01:12.127
They also respect the limits of judgment.

13
00:01:12.127 --> 00:01:15.722
Bias and noise are normal risks, not personal insults.

14
00:01:15.722 --> 00:01:27.287
Checklists, pre-mortems, red teams, independent estimates, decision records, and structured review help teams improve without pretending people are perfectly rational.

15
00:01:27.287 --> 00:01:35.883
In teams, role clarity and psychological safety make it more likely that the right information reaches the decision maker in time.

16
00:01:35.883 --> 00:01:42.369
The goal is better judgment, better process, better learning, and more reliable action over time.

17
00:01:42.369 --> 00:01:46.354
You will still make decisions with incomplete information.

18
00:01:46.354 --> 00:01:52.215
Some good decisions will still have bad outcomes, and some weak decisions will get lucky.

19
00:01:52.215 --> 00:01:56.044
Decision science gives you a way to keep improving anyway.
